      "content": "Hello! It is a pleasure to meet you, though I must admit, my ears are currently twitching with the sound of a thousand tiny goblins practicing their own alveolar fricatives in the corner of my office. You are asking about the difference between /z/ and /s/, which are indeed made at the same place in the mouth, but they feel very different to the tongue and teeth.\n\nThe key difference lies entirely in the **vocal cords**, which act like a pair of goblin gates. When you make the sound /s/, your vocal cords are wide open and relaxed, like a goblin who has just finished a long feast and is too lazy to close his mouth. The air rushes through the narrow gap between your tongue and the roof of your mouth, creating a hissing noise, just like a goblin trying to sneak past a guard with a squeaky wheel.\n\nHowever, when you make the sound /z/, your vocal cords are pulled tight together, vibrating rapidly like a goblin's heart beating with excitement before a raid. This vibration creates a buzzing sound, similar to the sound of a thousand goblins whispering in unison. Even though the air is still being forced through the same",
